e.\n</success_criteria>\n\n<output>\nCreate `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-SUMMARY.md` when done.\n</output>\n---\nphase: 01-retry-budget\nplan: 01\nsubsystem: probe\ntags: [python, retry-budget, unittest]\n\nrequires: []\nprovides:\n  - \"probe.budget.DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS = 120\"\n  - \"probe.budget.deadline_after(start_secs)\"\naffects: []\n\nactuals:\n  tokens: 110\n  tasks: 2\n  commits: 1\nplan_head_before: 0f16ce6a49303b714c17a7c0bb3a1d31b3b45cf3\nplan_head_after: 83b28a4\n\ntech-stack:\n  added: []\n  patterns: [\"module-level typed constant plus a pure helper function\"]\n\nkey-files:\n  created: [probe/__init__.py, probe/budget.py]\n  modified: []\n\nkey-decisions:\n  - \"DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS = 120 (option budget-120)\"\n\nrequirements-completed: [BUDGET-01]\n\ncoverage:\n  - id: D1\n    description: \"DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS is an int and one of the offered values (30, 120, 300); set to 120\"\n    requirement: \"BUDGET-01\"\n    verification:\n      - kind: unit\n        ref: \"tests/test_budget.py#test_budget_is_one_of_the_offered_values\"\n        status: pass\n    human_judgment: false\n  - id: D2\n    description: \"deadline_after adds the budget to a start time\"\n    requirement: \"BUDGET-01\"\n    verification:\n      - kind: unit\n        ref: \"tests/test_budget.py#test_deadline_adds_the_budget_to_the_start\"\n        status: pass\n    human_judgment: false\n\nduration: 5min\ncompleted: 2026-10-07\nstatus: complete\n---\n\n# Phase 1 Plan 01: Retry Budget Summary\n\n**`probe/budget.py` with a 120-second default retry budget and a `deadline_after` helper; the existing 2-test suite passes.**\n\n## Performance\n\n- **Duration:** about 5 min (continuation run, Task 2 only)\n- **Tasks:** 2 (Task 1 decision resolved by the orchestrator, Task 2 executed here)\n- **Files created:** 2\n\n## Accomplishments\n\n- Created `probe/__init__.py` (empty) and `probe/budget.py`.\n- `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int = 120`; `deadline_after(start_secs)` returns `start_secs + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS`.\n- `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` reports `Ran 2 tests` and `OK`. Before the module existed, the same command failed with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'probe'`.\n\n## Task Commits\n\n1. Task 1: Choose the default retry budget - decision only, no commit (resolved by orchestrator: `budget-120`)\n2. Task 2: Implement probe/budget.py - `83b28a4` (feat)\n\n## Decisions Made\n\n- **Default retry budget = 120 s (`budget-120`).** The upstream's failover completes within 90 s, so any budget below 90 s turns every failover into a hard failure (rules out 30). The on-call pager escalates a stalled job after 240 s, so a budget above that pages a human before the probe reports its own failure (rules out 300). 120 is the only offered value inside the 90-240 s window. Its cost, a worker slot held up to 2 min per stalled call, has no stated constraint against it. The decision was made by the orchestrator in an unattended DevFlow run that was explicitly resumed to resolve this gate (not by a human at the checkpoint).\n\n## Deviations from Plan\n\nNone - plan executed as written. Two environment notes, neither a code deviation:\n\n- The executor's standard pre-commit check requiring an `agent-*` branch name was not applied. The orchestrator pinned this sequential run to `feature/phase-01`, which is not a protected branch; the protected-branch check passed.\n- The run notes said `.planning/config.json` carried an uncommitted `_auto_chain_active: true` change. In this worktree `git status` showed no modification to it and the on-disk value is `false`. It was not staged or touched.\n\n## Verification and its limits\n\n- Both tests pass. Negative control: a throwaway membership check showed 120 is in the offered set and 999 is not. This checks the set-membership logic the test relies on; it did not run the real test against a bad value, so it does not prove the test itself fails on one.\n- The tests establish the v